# Local corpus variants dropped in for experiments. Thousands of files each.
#
# **The corpus the engine serves is no longer in here.** As of 2026-08-07 it is its own
# repository (D13) — ../BIRD-corpus, pointed at by GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR — because a
# served corpus that is untracked makes corpus_content_hash name bytes no reader can
# obtain, and every number measured against it unreproducible. That was recorded here as
# an open problem for the whole of v2 and is now closed for the served corpus.
#
# What is still ignored is what is still local: variant trees kept for comparison, and a
# seeded corpus written by from_live_schema. None of them can be rebuilt from anything
# committed — there is no curator module in src/ — so treat a variant as evidence only
# for as long as the directory survives. Promote it to the corpus repo before quoting a
# number from it. See docs/corpus-format.md.
corpora/

# Derived, rebuildable projections inside any corpus tree (ADR 0005 D9): search index,
# embeddings, compiled graph. Never authored directly, so never committed.
_generated/

# Firecrawl CLI scrape cache. The CLI writes it into the repo root, and AGENTS.md
# says the top level holds only README.md and configuration files.
.firecrawl/

# Vendored agent skills, fetched not authored. `skills-lock.json` pins each one by hash
# and IS committed — the same split as a package lock: the manifest is the source of
# truth, the tree beside it is the install. AGENTS.md names both.
.claude/skills/
.agents/

# Pre-v2 state parked before the rewrite: 293 MB of zipped corpora, runs and quarantine.
# Not history worth carrying in the repo, and large enough to make a clone painful.
_archive/

# One-off slices written while debugging the rebuild packets. The packets themselves are
# already ignored under scripts/corpus_rebuild/_build/; these are hand-cut copies of them.
scratch_*.json

# The frontend under `ui/` carries its own nested .gitignore, which is where node_modules,
# .next and .env.local are excluded. Nested is deliberate rather than folded in here: those
# patterns are relative to the app directory, and Next.js's own template is what generates
# them, so keeping them beside package.json means the next `create-next-app` diff is readable.
